.TH SH 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
sh \- command language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh
.br
.B sh
[-exE]
.I script
[\fIarg\fR ...]
.br
.B sh
.B -s
[\fIarg\fR ...]
.br
.B sh
[-exE]
.B -c
.I string
[\fIname\fR [\fIarg\fR ...]]
.br
.B sh
.B -E
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sh
is a command programming language that executes commands read from a
terminal or a file.
A simple command is a sequence of nonblank words separated by blanks
(a tab or a space). The first word specifies the name of the command;
the remaining words are passed as arguments.
The value of a command is its exit status.
.PP
.B Invocation.
With no operands sh reads standard input, prompting if it is a terminal.
The first operand after the options -e (exit on error) and -x (trace)
names a script file, leaving standard input free for that script's
commands; $0 is the script's path and the operands after it are $1,
$2 and so on.
With -s the commands come from standard input and every operand is a
positional parameter ($0 stays sh).
With -c the first operand is a command string, run as one line with no
prompt and no line editor, and sh then exits with the status of the last
command (usage error, status 2, if the string is missing); the next
operand, if any, is $0 and the operands after it are $1, $2 and so on,
as in POSIX sh -c.
The string is a single line: a newline inside it is not a command
separator.
Standard input is untouched, so the string's commands read it.
.PP
A script file may be in the system's standard text form (ProDOS TXT:
low ASCII bytes, a carriage return $0D ending each line) as well as
sh's own high-bit form; it runs as written.
This holds for a file named as the script, a file read by the . command
and a file redirected to standard input, and not for text arriving on a
pipe or typed at a terminal.
.PP
.B Simple commands.
A command may be prefixed by assignments, NAME=value words before the
command name: they are expanded like an ordinary assignment (no field
splitting, no globbing), in order, and exported for that command only.
The shell's own variables are the same afterwards, including a
variable's export mark, so TERM=vt100 vi file starts vi with that TERM.
For a builtin the assignment holds while it runs (IFS=: read a b).
The assignments of a special builtin (:, ., break, continue, eval, exit,
export, readonly, set, shift, times, trap, unset, and exec with no
command) persist in the shell,
and so do those of a command that has no name (A=1 B=2, or A=1 > file).
Each stage of a pipeline takes its own.
A NAME=value word after the command name is an ordinary argument, and
there is no -k.
Eight variables is the limit: an assignment that needs a ninth, or
that names a readonly variable, stops the command with a message.
A name that does not fit the kernel's argument region is refused with
a message and the command does not run.
.PP
.B Line editing.
When standard input is the console and sh is reading a command at its own
prompt, sh edits the line itself, with the console in raw mode for the
length of that one line (a $( ) substitution, a script, a sourced file,
a pipe or sh < file is read as before, and every command sh starts runs
with the console cooked again).
Bindings: Control-A and Control-E go to the start and end of the line;
Control-B, Control-F and the left and right arrows move a character;
Open Apple plus f or b (or ESC f, ESC b) move a word; Control-P, Control-N
and the up and down arrows walk the history; Open Apple plus k (or ESC k),
or Option plus k, deletes to the end of the line and Option plus u deletes
the whole line; Backspace and DEL erase before the cursor;
Control-D deletes the character under the cursor, or ends the shell on an
empty line; Control-L clears the screen and redraws the prompt and the
line at the top.
Unlike the kernel's cooked editor, Control-L does not bring back output
printed before the prompt (a process cannot read the kernel's copy of it),
as in bash and zsh; sh -E keeps the old behaviour.
A physical line typed at the editor is at most 127 characters.
A logical line is at most 255 bytes (below).
The history holds the last eight logical lines (as many as fit in 512
bytes), is private to each shell, and is not kept when it exits: a nested
sh and a shell started afresh begin with none. A line is added once it is
complete, whole, as a single entry; one longer than 127 bytes is not added.
Recalling an entry that spans several lines paints a real line break, so
editing it can leave the cursor off by a row; the line itself is right.
.PP
A quote left open at the end of a line, or a backslash as the last
character of a line outside single quotes, continues the command onto the
next line. At the console sh shows a secondary prompt, > , until the
command is complete; a script, a sourced file or a pipe prints nothing.
Inside quotes the newline is part of the word; a backslash-newline outside
single quotes is removed. A # that begins a word starts a comment, and
nothing in a comment continues the line. Control-C at the secondary prompt
abandons the whole command (status 130); Control-D gives
.B sh: unterminated quote
and returns to the primary prompt. Quotes inside an open $( ) or ( ) do not
continue the line. A logical line longer than 255 bytes is refused whole,
with
.BR "sh: line too long" ,
status 1, and the rest of the command it spans is skipped.
The editor assumes the prompt starts in column 0; after output that ends
without a newline the prompt follows it on the same row, and an edit that
crosses a row boundary on that line can leave the display untidy though
the line itself, and what runs, are right.
.PP
.B Completion.
TAB completes the word before the cursor and TAB TAB (no key between)
lists the candidates. In command position the candidates are the
aliases (matched in exact case), the builtins and reserved words and the
runnable files of every PATH directory (types BIN, SYS and TXT; a TXT
without a #! line does not run, so this is a superset), listed once
each however many places name them; elsewhere, and after a < or > and
for the words of a for list, they are the names in the directory the
word names, including / , /dev and /mnt. A unique match is completed
with a / after a directory and a blank after anything else (no blank if
one already follows); several matches complete as far as they all agree,
and TAB again, with no key between, prints them sorted in columns,
at most 31 and then a ... row, and redraws the prompt and the line.
Only a suffix of what was typed is inserted, in the spelling of the
first candidate. The cursor must stand at the end of a word: the byte
after it may only be the end of the line, a blank or one of | ; & < > ( ).
Nothing is done (the line is left alone, with no bell) for a word that
holds a quote, backslash, $, backquote, glob character or =, in a
comment, after << or >& or <&, as the NAME of a for, on a > continuation
line, or if a directory cannot be read. Assignment words before the
command (A=1 ca) do not change which word is the command. Variables,
~ and options are not completed, and neither is anything in vi or ex.
.PP
.B Interrupts at the prompt.
Control-C at the prompt abandons the line being typed, and Control-Z does
the same (it never stops the shell).
They reach the shell alone: a background job, or a stopped job, is not
touched by them.
If a trap is set on INT (or on signal 14), that trap runs, as in other
shells, before the next prompt.
A trap on any other signal runs at once if the line is empty and no loop
or if is open (at a > prompt it waits), and after the line if something
has been typed.
With the terminal's echo off (stty -echo) nothing may show as the line
is typed, so the kernel's cooked editor reads that line, as with -E.
The
.B -E
option turns the line editor off; the kernel's cooked line editor then
reads the prompt, with the same bindings (there, Control-D on a non-empty
line ends it without a newline instead of deleting forward).
.PP
.B Signals.
An interactive shell (standard input is a terminal) ignores TERM, as in
V7, so that a stray TERM, such as one from a script, does not end it;
kill -9 still does, and on the console init starts a new shell.
A trap on signal 15 takes precedence, and trap - 15 restores the ignore.
The shell's children do not inherit it, so they die of TERM as usual.
A login shell, the one init starts, also sets the terminal to ICANON,
ECHO and OPOST when it starts; a shell started from another shell leaves
the terminal as it finds it.
A text file whose first line is #!interpreter [arg] is run by the shell
as interpreter [arg] script args, so it may be named as a command; an
interpreter that is itself such a script is followed once.
Other programs that spawn commands (find -exec, xargs, env, time) do
not do this and report that they cannot execute the file.
.PP
.B Commands and lists.
A pipeline is a sequence of commands separated by |.
The standard output of each command but the last is connected by a pipe
to the standard input of the next.
At most three stages are supported.
Separate commands with a newline or ;.
Use && to continue only after success, || only after failure,
and a trailing & to start a background job.
{ list; } groups commands in this shell, not a new process.
.PP
.B Quoting and expansion.
Single quotes protect their contents literally.
Double quotes allow dollar expansion but protect spaces.
A backslash quotes the following character; inside double quotes it is
special before $, backquote, double quote, backslash and newline.
Quoted and unquoted fragments can form one word.
# begins a comment at the start of an unquoted word.
.PP
$NAME and \fB${NAME}\fR expand variables; NAME=value assigns one.
$0 through $9 are positional parameters, $# their count,
$? the last status, $$ the shell process ID and $! the last background PID.
$* and $@ expand positional parameters.
The ${V-word}, ${V=word}, ${V?word} and ${V+word} forms, and their
colon variants, provide defaults, assignment, errors or alternatives.
${V#pattern}, ${V##pattern}, ${V%pattern} and ${V%%pattern}
remove a shortest/longest matching prefix or suffix.
Unquoted expansions split on IFS; its default is space, tab and newline.
Unquoted * ? and bracket patterns select file names.
Only the last component of a path is matched, so a/*/b is not
expanded; the directories /, /dev and /mnt match like any other.
.PP
$(command args) substitutes the output of a command.
The body is run by a nested sh, so pipelines, redirections, lists and
background operators inside it mean what they say, as in
$(ls | wc -l).
What is refused, with a diagnostic and a nonzero status, is nesting
(any ( inside the body, so $( $( ) ) and $( ( ) ) both fail) and a $(
with no closing ).
Backquote substitution, shell functions and $((arithmetic)) are absent;
use expr(1) for arithmetic.
.PP
.B Redirection.
< file reads input; > file replaces output; >> file appends.
N> file and N>> file redirect descriptor N (0 to 7), and N< file opens
file for reading on descriptor N; 2> file redirects diagnostics and 0< is <.
Redirections are applied from left to right, so
.B cmd > out 2> err
sends each stream to its own file and
.B cmd 2>&1 > out
leaves diagnostics on the old standard output; each stage of a pipeline
applies its own after the pipe is connected, and
.B exec
applies them to the shell itself in the same order. A loop or group
terminator (done, fi, }) takes one output redirection.
Descriptor duplication and closing use
forms such as 2>&1, 3<&0, >&- and <&-.
Here-documents use <<word or <<-word; the latter strips leading tabs.
A quoted delimiter suppresses body expansion.
.PP
.B Busy files.
ProDOS lets a file be open only once when anyone may write it, so a >
or >> whose target another process holds open at that instant (a
.B tail -f
between its polls, a short
.BR cp )
is retried: the shell waits one tick and tries again, up to 30 times
(about a second), and ^C ends the wait with status 130. Past the bound,
or for any other error, it prints
.B sh: cannot redirect.
This is not Unix, where an open never waits: a long holder
.RB ( "exec 3<file; echo x >> file" )
still fails, after the bound instead of at once.
Here-documents are limited to 255 bytes and the first pipeline stage.
.PP
.B Control flow.
The shell implements if/then/elif/else/fi, while/do/done,
until/do/done, for name in words/do/done, and case/in/esac.
Use shell patterns and ;; to separate case arms.
Loops rewind a seekable script; console loop input is spooled to a
temporary file. Arbitrary pipe-fed loop input is not a seekable script.
For example, in a command file:
.PP
.B for f in *.S
.br
.B do
.br
.B "    echo $f"
.br
.B done
.PP
.B Builtin commands.
Builtins and reserved words are recognized without regard to case.
Most builtins require a single-command pipeline.
They are not generally usable as external commands.
.TP
.B :
Do nothing successfully.
.TP
.B . file
Read commands from file in the current shell; nesting is limited to four.
.TP
.B cd directory
Change the working directory.
.TP
.B echo [-n] [word ...]
Print words separated by spaces. -n omits the newline.
An external echo is used for unsupported builtin I/O shapes.
.TP
.B test expression, [ expression ]
Test a condition; the bracket form requires its closing ].
-z/-n test string length; =/!= compare strings.
-e/-f/-d test existence, file and directory; -r/-w test ProDOS
read/write access; -x tests a BIN or SYS type, not an execute bit.
-s tests allocated blocks, not byte-exact length.
-eq/-ne/-lt/-le/-gt/-ge compare unsigned numbers.
! negates; -a/-o combine conditions; quote parentheses for grouping.
Statuses are 0 true, 1 false, 2 invalid expression.
.TP
.B break [n], continue [n]
Leave loops or begin their next iteration.
.TP
.B shift [n]
Discard leading positional parameters.
.TP
.B export [name[=value] ...]
Mark variables for inheritance by external commands.
.TP
.B unset name ...
Remove variables; readonly variables cannot be removed.
.TP
.B readonly [-p] [name[=value] ...]
Protect variables from assignment. Without names, list them.
The listing does not quote values containing blanks.
.TP
.B read name ...
Read one input line, splitting it among variables using IFS;
the last receives the remainder. EOF returns nonzero.
A direct 'read name < file' is refused; use a compound input redirection
or a script file distinct from standard input.
.TP
.B getopts optstring name [arg ...]
Parse short options, setting name, OPTARG and OPTIND.
A colon after an option letter requires an argument; a leading colon
selects silent error reporting. Set OPTIND=1 to restart.
Without explicit arguments, use positional parameters.
.TP
.B set -x, set +x, set -e, set +e
Enable/disable tracing or exit-on-error.
-e does not exit for commands used as conditions or tested by &&/||.
These are the supported set forms, not a general options interface.
.TP
.B command [-v|-V] name [arg ...], type name ...
Run an external command, or report command lookup.
-v is terse; -V and type give descriptions.
.TP
.B eval [arg ...]
Join arguments with spaces and execute the resulting command text.
.TP
.B exec [command [arg ...]]
With only redirections, change the shell's own descriptors permanently.
The descriptor the shell reads its commands from (a script, a sourced
file, a construct's redirect) is kept high, from 7 down, so exec 3<file
in a script does not touch it; an exec onto one of those descriptors is
refused with exec: cannot redirect.
With a command, run it externally and then exit with its status.
Prefix assignments are exported to it.
This does not replace the shell process: its bank remains occupied
while the child runs.
.TP
.B exit [status]
Leave the shell, using the given status or the last command's status.
.TP
.B trap [command] [signal ...]
Run command when a held signal is taken between commands.
An empty command ignores signals; a signal-first form, or - as the
command, restores defaults.
With no operands, list traps. EXIT or 0 selects the exit trap.
Names HUP, INT, QUIT, KILL and TERM are recognized, but KILL cannot be
caught or ignored.
A trap on INT also runs when Control-C is typed at the idle prompt.
.TP
.B jobs, fg [job], bg [job], wait [pid ...]
List jobs, resume in foreground/background, or wait for children.
Control-C interrupts the foreground job; Control-Z stops it.
A foreground command killed by a signal other than the interrupt and the
broken pipe is named on the standard error as V7 sh named it (Killed,
Terminated, Trace/BPT trap, Hangup, ...), and the same names label a
finished background job; the status is 128 plus the signal number.
Use jobs to identify a stopped job before resuming it.
.TP
.B alias [name[=value] ...], unalias [-a] name ...
With no operands alias lists every alias as alias name='value'.
A name alone prints that alias (or "alias: name: not found",
status 1); name=value defines one. unalias removes the named
aliases, or all with -a. Only the first word of a command is
replaced, text-for-text, before the line is parsed: after the
start of a line, or after ; & && || | ( { ! if then elif else
while until do. A name being expanded is not expanded again
inside its own value, and a value ending in a blank makes the
NEXT word eligible too. A word quoted in any part (\(aqls\(aq, \\ls) is never
an alias. Names are case-sensitive, 15 characters of letters,
digits and . _ - ! % , @ + : ; reserved words are refused.
The table holds EIGHT aliases of at most 31 characters each; a
ninth, a longer value, or an expansion that would pass the
255-character line limit is an error, never a truncation.
Aliases are not inherited by a subshell (( ), $( ) or a script
run by sh): each shell starts with none. Divergences: a word
after VAR=value or a case pattern is not checked, and an alias
may not stand before a for/while/until head on the same typed
line.
.TP
.B hash [-r] [name ...]
sh keeps a tracked-command cache of FOUR resolved commands.
hash -r empties it. hash name resolves name as a spawn would
(PATH search, or a probe if it contains a slash), records it,
and is silent with status 0; if it cannot be found it prints
"hash: name: not found" and returns 1 (a builtin name is not a
PATH command, so hash cd fails, as in bash). Bare hash prints
nothing and returns 0: the listing format POSIX leaves open is
not implemented.
.TP
.B fc
Not supported: the command history belongs to the console's line
editor and no system call exposes it. Prints "sh: fc: not
supported (history is the console's)" and returns 2.
.TP
.B times
Print two pairs of time fields. Only the first is measured: elapsed
time since shell startup, NOT user CPU time. The other three are zero.
With no usable tick source all four are zero.
.PP
type, times, alias, unalias, hash, readonly listings, command -v/-V
and eval refuse direct
redirections. exec is the explicit descriptor-changing exception.
.PP
External commands are located using PATH, except that a command word
containing a slash is never searched: it is a path, taken relative to
the current directory unless it begins with a slash, so ./prog, ../prog
and dir/prog run the program named (a . or .. in the middle of the
word, dir/../prog, is not resolved). A command that cannot be found
returns status 127. ProDOS file names are
case-insensitive and each component has at most fifteen characters.
